<section class="section wrap about-me" id="about-me" aria-labelledby="about-me-title"><div class="intro-simple">
 <h2 id="about-me-title">Hi, I’m<br/><span>Min!</span></h2>
 <div class="intro-copy"><p>I’m an <strong>AI Governance Analyst at Krafton</strong> and a graduating senior at the University of Illinois Urbana-Champaign (UIUC).</p>
 <p>I prepared this page for my interviews for the <strong>Strategic Projects Associate, Safety role at Handshake AI</strong>.</p>
 <p class="intro-bridge">Here’s my understanding of Handshake’s business, the role, and how my experience in AI governance and operation fits.</p></div>
</div></section>

<HandshakeBusiness />
<HandshakeNarrative />
<HandshakeCollaboration />
<HandshakePipelines />

<section class="section wrap personal-statement" id="personal-statement" aria-labelledby="personal-statement-title">
 <div class="personal-note"><HandshakeArt variant="note" />
  <header class="panel-heading"><p class="label">A PERSONAL NOTE</p><h2 id="personal-statement-title">Why I want to work<br/>closer to human data.</h2></header>
  <div class="personal-copy personal-motivation">
   <p>I keep thinking about <em>The Three-Body Problem</em>, where a human tries to explain people to an intelligence far more advanced than our own. What stayed with me was the effort to <mark>help that intelligence understand us</mark>, rather than make it more capable. That is what interests me about human data: helping AI understand what people mean, including the context and intentions that aren’t stated directly.</p>
   <p>At Krafton, I worked on helping employees use AI safely and helping the company respond to changes in the technology. At Pacvue, I supported internal enablement at a company using AI to improve its SaaS offering. Both experiences taught me about putting AI to work inside a business. The goals were tied to each company’s operations, and <mark>my work did not directly shape frontier models</mark>.</p>
   <p>I want my next role to bring me closer to that process. When I first learned about supervised learning, I thought of data as a way to learn more about the world and make better decisions. Since then, I’ve become more interested in how it can help models understand human nuance and behave more safely. Handshake’s work with frontier labs feels like a way to contribute to that: bringing what I’ve learned about review processes and AI use into <mark>the work of creating and evaluating human data</mark>.</p>
  </div>
  <footer><span>Min Kim</span></footer>
 </div>
</section>


</main>
